{
  "dom_id": "step-q53747",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y))+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(y)}{x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
